---
import Shot from "../components/Shot.astro";
import ReleaseBlock from "../components/ReleaseBlock.astro";
import { url } from "../paths";
---

<div class="shell-page edit-page">
  <section class="band shell-intro">
    <h1>Rivet Edit</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      A small code editor for a folder, or for a few files on their own. It uses the same colors,
      fonts, menus, Preferences, and quick panel as the other Rivet apps.
    </p>
    <p>
      It is still young. Keep a copy of anything you care about. Keys and the build are in the
      <a href={url("docs/edit/")}>Edit docs</a>.
    </p>
    <Shot
      name="edit-editor-wide.png"
      alt="Rivet Edit with the hello-rivet folder open, the file tree on the left, and main.rs in the editor"
      caption="A folder open. The tree is on the left, the file in the middle."
      eager
    />
  </section>

  <section class="band edit-files" aria-labelledby="edit-files">
    <h2 id="edit-files">A folder, or a file</h2>
    <p>
      Open a folder and its files sit in a tree. The tree leaves out <code>.git</code>,
      <code>node_modules</code>, <code>target</code>, and a few other tool and build folders. You
      can also open single files, each in its own tab.
    </p>
    <p>
      One click in the tree, or Go to File, opens a preview. The tab title is in italics, and the
      next preview takes its place. Double-click the file, Ctrl+click it, double-click the tab, or
      change the text, and the tab stays.
    </p>
    <p>
      Rust, Python, JavaScript, TypeScript, JSON, shell, C, Go, HTML, CSS, TOML, and YAML are
      colored with tree-sitter. Other files use the same colors as Peek in Rivet Files. Those
      colors follow the theme and the accent.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="band edit-quick" aria-labelledby="edit-quick">
    <h2 id="edit-quick">Find a file, or a command</h2>
    <p>
      Ctrl+P finds a file from a few letters of its name or its path. Shift+Ctrl+P is the command
      list. Ctrl+G jumps to a line. The same quick panel is in the other apps.
    </p>
    <div class="shell-grid">
      <Shot
        name="edit-go-to-file-wide.png"
        alt="Go to File in Rivet Edit, with a short search and matching paths"
        caption="Go to File."
      />
      <Shot
        name="edit-commands-wide.png"
        alt="The Rivet Edit command list, opened with Shift+Ctrl+P"
        caption="Commands."
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band edit-look" aria-labelledby="edit-look">
    <h2 id="edit-look">Desktop colors, or a code theme</h2>
    <p>
      By default the editor follows the app, which follows your desktop. Preferences can turn that
      off. Color Theme, in the command list, switches the code to one of 39 themes, including One
      Dark, Dracula, Catppuccin, Tokyo Night, Nord, Gruvbox, and Rosé Pine. Moving through the list
      previews each one.
    </p>
    <div class="shell-grid">
      <Shot
        name="edit-preferences-wide.png"
        alt="Rivet Edit Preferences on Appearance, set to follow the desktop colors, with the pink accent selected"
        caption="Preferences, following the desktop."
      />
      <Shot
        name="edit-light-wide.png"
        alt="Rivet Edit in the light theme, with a JavaScript file open"
        caption="The light theme."
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band edit-git" aria-labelledby="edit-git">
    <h2 id="edit-git">Git, find, and the rest</h2>
    <p>
      The sidebar has a Git tab. It shows the branch, a commit message, conflicts, staged changes,
      and changes not staged yet. Changed files are colored in the tree, and the gutter marks what
      changed since you staged. It runs your own git, so your config, hooks, and sign-in apply.
    </p>
    <p>
      Find and replace work in the open file, and search can cover the whole folder. Suggestions
      show up as you type. You can add another cursor, on the next copy of a word or on the line
      above. F12 goes to a definition. Vim keys stay off until you turn them on in Preferences.
    </p>
    <p>A panel item for Rivet Edit is coming soon.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="band edit-download">
    <ReleaseBlock slug="edit" heading="h2" />
  </section>
</div>
